Event-Driven Architecture (EDA)
Event-Driven Architecture is an architecture where different components/services communicate by producing and reacting to events.
Something happens → an event is generated → interested services react to that event.

An event simply represents something that has already happened, such as:
OrderPlaced
PaymentCompleted
UserRegistered
ProductShipped

Simple example
Suppose a user places an order:
                 OrderPlaced Event
Order Service ─────────┬───────────────
                       │
              Message Broker
                 /      |       \
                ↓       ↓        ↓
          Inventory   Email    Analytics
           Service   Service    Service

The Order Service publishes:
OrderPlaced
{
   orderId: 123,
   userId: 456
}

Then different services independently react:
Inventory Service → Reduce stock
Email Service     → Send confirmation
Analytics Service → Record order

The Order Service doesn't need to directly call every service.
Traditional vs Event-Driven
Synchronous/direct communication:
Order Service
    ├──→ Inventory Service
    ├──→ Email Service
    └──→ Analytics Service

Order Service is more directly coupled to these interactions.
Event-driven:
Order Service
      ↓
 "OrderPlaced"
      ↓
 Message Broker
   ↙    ↓    ↘
Inventory Email Analytics

Services can be more loosely coupled.
Main components
Producer → generates/publishes an event.
Event Broker / Message Broker → transports/stores/routes events depending on the technology.
Consumer → receives an event and performs some action.
Producer → Broker → Consumer(s)